answers.workaround:
For some of the information, it's possible to get it directly from the UI. For example:
```gdscript
	var viewport = get_editor_interface().get_editor_viewport()
	var editor = viewport.get_child(0)
	var snapdialog : Node
	for i in editor.get_children():
		if i.is_class("SnapDialog"):
			snapdialog = i
			break
	var grid_settings = snapdialog.get_child(3).get_child(0)
	snap_offset = Vector2(grid_settings.get_child(1).value, grid_settings.get_child(2).value)
	snap_step = Vector2(grid_settings.get_child(4).value, grid_settings.get_child(5).value)
```
Setting aside how hacky it is, it's also unreliable, since the data in the dialog does not always match the current settings, as hitting "cancel" on the dialog will not cause the input values to reset until the dialog is reopened.

And this is just for one piece of information of the countless details vailable from get_editor_states()
